WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.010 --> 00:00:04.620
- Hello and welcome to
today's CIFAR Virtual Talk,

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.520
part of our series which spotlights

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:08.600
how research from across the academy

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.180
from computer science,
population and public health,

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:13.690
economics, and anthropology amongst others

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:15.450
may be used as the basis

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:17.160
for evidence informed decision making

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.163
in the fight against COVID-19.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:21.650
If you're not familiar with us

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:24.814
CIFAR is a Canadian-based
global research organization

00:00:24.814 --> 00:00:27.080
that convenes extraordinary minds

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:29.160
to address the most important questions

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:31.660
facing science and humanity.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:33.150
My name is Rachel Parker.

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:35.240
I'm senior director of research at CIFAR.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:37.370
It's my distinct pleasure to introduce you

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:38.643
to professor Brian Dias.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:42.460
Brian joined the faculty

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:43.910
of the Keck School of Medicine

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:46.120
at the University of Southern
California this fall,

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:49.240
having made a cross country
move from Emory University

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:50.756
in Atlanta with his family

00:00:50.756 --> 00:00:53.203
in the midst of the COVID pandemic.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.680
His research seeks to
understand how neurobiology,

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:58.880
physiology, and reproductive biology

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:01.010
are impacted by stress or trauma

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:02.900
and how legacies of stress or trauma

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:06.180
experienced by parents
impact their children.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.720
Brian was a CIFAR Azrieli Global Scholar

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.280
and is now a member of CIFAR's program

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.570
on child and brain development.

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:14.900
In addition to research,

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:17.290
Brian has sought to demystify neuroscience

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:19.650
and communicate the value
of science to society

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:22.160
in both traditional and
nontraditional classrooms

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:23.220
around the world.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:26.100
Including, of course, he has
taught to Tibetan Buddhist

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:29.063
monks at monasteries
across India since 2013.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:31.020
Before we begin,

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.540
today's session will last
approximately 30 minutes.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.200
I'll be asking Brian some questions

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.410
before opening it up to the audience.

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:38.530
If you do have a question,

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:41.350
you can use the Q and A
function to submit one.

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:43.562
And lastly, the session is being recorded

00:01:43.562 --> 00:01:46.940
and will be available on
the CIFAR website next week.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:48.713
Brian, thanks so much for joining.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.660
So your research is something
that seems so very relevant

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:58.530
to 2020 in terms of the
lasting impacts of the pandemic

00:01:58.530 --> 00:01:59.630
and other social unrest

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:01.680
that we've been
experiencing or witnessing.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.410
What does intergenerational
trauma really mean?

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:08.130
How does it transmit from
one generation to the next?

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:11.320
- Historical events and data
collected in the aftermath

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:15.570
of those events suggests that
atrocities that have occurred

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:18.730
across history not only
affect the population

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:20.950
of the generation that's
directly experienced

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:24.060
those atrocities or
environmental catastrophes,

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:25.750
but also future generations.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:29.250
So really quickly, one of those
examples is the Holocaust.

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:32.150
Some descendants of Holocaust survivors

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:34.640
would go on to develop PTSD and anxiety

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:36.090
at a higher incidence

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:39.593
than descendants of Holocaust survivors

00:02:39.593 --> 00:02:43.057
who did develop PTSD in the
aftermath of the Holocaust.

00:02:43.057 --> 00:02:45.230
Even though these descendants
may not have experienced

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:46.670
the Holocaust themselves.

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:47.730
And there have been famines

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:48.600
that have occurred across history,

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.873
most notably the Dutch
Hunger Winter in 1944,

00:02:51.873 --> 00:02:54.550
that suggests to us that exposure

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:58.020
to nutritional insults not
only affects the population

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:01.830
of the generation that's
exposed that nutritional famine,

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:04.870
but also future generations
who are not even conceived

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:05.780
at the time.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:09.060
So that suggests to us that
there are legacies of trauma

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:10.950
that start off in one generation

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:13.340
and then perpetuate across generations.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:16.320
And our challenge is to try and
understand how that happens.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:19.230
And right now we're thinking
of three potential mechanisms

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:20.670
by which that occurs.

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:23.770
So at a very basic level,

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:27.230
we can think about social transmission,

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:29.510
talking at the dinner
table about the Holocaust,

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:31.680
about racism, about poverty

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:33.580
and that gets embedded under the skin

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:35.367
to then allow for not only the generation

00:03:35.367 --> 00:03:40.140
that directly experienced those
atrocities to be affected,

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:41.933
but also future generations.

00:03:41.933 --> 00:03:45.642
But we're also gaining a
really important perspective

00:03:45.642 --> 00:03:47.800
in thinking that sperm and egg

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:51.060
might also bear imprints of trauma

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:53.720
in the generation that's
directly exposed to it,

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.890
thereby allowing for embryonic development

00:03:56.890 --> 00:04:00.250
to then be affected and future
generations to be affected.

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:02.620
And of course there's
also the really important

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:04.750
maternal fetal interface,

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:07.780
where if there's a stressor
while the mom is pregnant,

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:11.210
the placenta serves as
a mode of communication

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:12.830
between fetus and mom,

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:14.770
and the fetus then bears imprints

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:17.050
of that intergenerational trauma.

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:18.020
And so that's how we're thinking

00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:20.180
about intergenerational trauma right now,

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:22.380
and some of the mechanisms
that underlie it.

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:25.930
- So, in thinking about trauma then,

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:27.680
maybe not to ask the obvious question,

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:29.567
but what does science tell us about COVID

00:04:29.567 --> 00:04:32.167
and how it's turned our
world upside down this year?

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:36.150
- Yeah, so in the stress field

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:38.810
there is wealth of literature

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:41.520
talking about two concepts

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:44.330
called controllability and predictability.

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:48.710
And so the idea being that
our stress is predictable

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:52.160
or unpredictable, is
controllable or not controllable.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:53.297
And so you might be saying,

00:04:53.297 --> 00:04:56.620
"How can stress ever be
controllable and predictable"?

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:58.570
And so for that, I'll give you an example

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:00.860
where you might have children in school

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:02.730
and they know that
they're going to be tested

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:04.410
at set periods of time.

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:06.230
And so one can control that stress.

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:09.250
So by preparation, it's
a predictable stress,

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:11.280
because you know when that's coming on.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:14.960
Unfortunately with COVID, we're
firmly in the unpredictable

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:16.700
and the uncontrollable realm,

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:21.690
and that's actually piling
on misery like no other

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:24.710
- So given that perspective then,

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:28.206
could the COVID-19 pandemic
or other experiences

00:05:28.206 --> 00:05:30.760
that we're witnessing this year

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:33.653
from the perspective of an
entire generation of children,

00:05:35.007 --> 00:05:38.370
thinking about economic strain
on the family, for example,

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:40.320
serve as a risk factor for the type

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:44.193
of intergenerational trauma
and stress that you study?

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:47.040
- I think before we answer that question

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:50.395
we should take a moment to
think about how are children

00:05:50.395 --> 00:05:54.900
and adults affected by COVID
from a phenomenological level.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:56.580
And so just some of these ideas

00:05:57.491 --> 00:05:59.450
are with respect to infants and toddlers.

00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:01.220
There's so much unpredictability

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:04.440
with daycares shutting down,
with teachers being let go,

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:07.120
and we know that children
need that consistency,

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:09.800
that predictability to be able to develop.

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:11.740
Then there's all this mask wearing

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:13.630
which is really important for us

00:06:13.630 --> 00:06:16.480
to be able to be social beings

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:18.960
and gain social cues from our caregivers.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:20.560
And so there's that unpredictability

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:23.180
that comes at infants and toddlers.

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:26.260
There's also then when
you talk about elementary

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:27.990
and middle school children

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:30.770
how social interactions
are a key component

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:33.150
of developing impulse control,

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:36.760
of developing a stable
social relationships.

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:39.820
Having those masks is not helping that.

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:41.440
I know it's been said that eyes

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:42.707
are the windows to one's soul,

00:06:42.707 --> 00:06:45.070
but as everyone can attest to right now

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:47.660
having these masks and not realizing

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:51.150
all these facial expressions
carry so much emotion,

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:53.700
it's difficult then for children to learn

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:57.120
what appropriate versus
inappropriate information is.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:59.880
There's also challenges
which we need to think about

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:02.650
with respect to language development.

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:05.460
Speaking over masks is
gonna change intonation,

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:07.870
is gonna change how language is perceived.

00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:09.630
And we know that children learn language

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:12.713
in some way by imitating
what they're hearing.

00:07:12.713 --> 00:07:15.310
Of course, then you talk
about high schoolers

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:18.510
and undergraduates and how
their lives have been upended

00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:20.390
with college applications

00:07:20.390 --> 00:07:22.740
and not knowing what the future holds.

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:26.570
And a critical period called adolescence,

00:07:26.570 --> 00:07:29.750
where you're developing impulse control,

00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:32.900
and you can't try all those things out,

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:35.610
as you're not in these
social situations again.

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:37.273
Then, we need to talk about parenting

00:07:37.273 --> 00:07:41.580
and how the toll of being maybe let go,

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:45.550
the toll of staying in the same house

00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:47.930
in cooped up conditions might result

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:49.780
in physical and emotional abuse

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:51.800
and how that's gonna affect children.

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:54.790
And then last but not least,
to talk about nutrition.

00:07:54.790 --> 00:07:57.580
And there's been a lot of
talk about food insecurity

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:01.750
and there are epochs
in time, early infancy,

00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:05.540
and then adolescents where the
brain really needs nutrition

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:09.130
to be able to develop in
a normative LD manner,

00:08:09.130 --> 00:08:10.600
and children are not getting that.

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:12.740
Some children are not
getting that right now.

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:15.530
And so that creates the storm

00:08:15.530 --> 00:08:19.420
which then could perpetuate for
several generations to come.

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:21.930
Now we have no idea how COVID

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:24.170
is going to affect not only the children

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:26.490
who are directly exposed to COVID,

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:28.290
but also future generations.

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:32.120
But if history suggest anything,

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:33.750
it suggests that it will.

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:37.364
And we know this because one
of the most well known studies

00:08:37.364 --> 00:08:40.480
called the Advanced Childhood Exposures,

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:43.450
Childhood Experience Study
has shown that exposure

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:45.510
to adverse childhood experiences,

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:48.313
like emotional abuse, like
the separation of parents

00:08:48.313 --> 00:08:52.930
like divorce, that will
increase the incidence

00:08:52.930 --> 00:08:55.560
of diseases like cardiovascular disease,

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:58.620
diabetes in the children

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:01.430
as well as when they
grow onto become adults.

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:04.190
And then of course, there's
the data which I talked about

00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:06.680
about intergenerational
legacies of trauma.

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:09.740
And I'm thinking, for
example, about the 1999

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:13.010
ice storm in Canada,

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:15.220
the famines that have
occurred across history,

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:17.430
all of those suggests that what happens

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:20.810
to one generation can
affect future generations.

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:22.950
And so if history is anything to go by,

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:25.490
COVID will leave lasting imprints

00:09:25.490 --> 00:09:28.350
not only on the degeneration
that's directly exposed

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:30.140
but also future generations

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:35.750
- Brian, that paints a fairly
gloomy picture for society.

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:39.563
Is there a silver lining?

00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:44.280
- As gloomy as that picture is,

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:47.130
we have to hold onto silver linings.

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:49.240
And thankfully science says

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:51.700
that there are silver linings.

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:53.894
The die is not cast,

00:09:53.894 --> 00:09:57.550
These influences of
trauma on the generation

00:09:57.550 --> 00:09:58.920
that's directly exposed to them,

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:02.430
but also future generations can be halted,

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:04.920
they can be reversed, and that's our goal

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:07.320
as a society to be able to do that.

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:10.973
So how should we be thinking
about how that occurs?

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:13.810
One way by which we should
be thinking about that

00:10:13.810 --> 00:10:16.220
is the importance of
nurturing environments.

00:10:16.220 --> 00:10:17.747
And so for that,

00:10:17.747 --> 00:10:21.320
I'll point you to the Bucharest
Early Intervention Project

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:24.780
which was pioneered amongst
others by Dr. Charles Nelson

00:10:24.780 --> 00:10:27.490
who's part of the CIFAR Child
and Brain Development Program.

00:10:27.490 --> 00:10:30.680
And what they found studying children

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:34.340
who are often when the
Ceausescu regime fell in Romania

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:37.250
and those children, some of them were put

00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:41.210
in really impoverished conditions
in institutionalized care,

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:43.480
what the Bucharest Early
Intervention Project showed

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:45.400
is that if you can take
some of those children

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:48.710
and put them in nurturing
environments with foster care,

00:10:48.710 --> 00:10:52.540
you can reverse the derailment
of neuro development,

00:10:52.540 --> 00:10:55.680
you could reverse the physiological
ailments that persist,

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:58.739
you can reverse the emotional
disorders that occur

00:10:58.739 --> 00:11:00.670
in those children.

00:11:00.670 --> 00:11:02.440
And so the die is not cast.

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:03.830
I keep coming back to that

00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:06.090
because you can reverse those imprints

00:11:06.090 --> 00:11:09.140
of an impoverished childhood.

00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:12.820
So then, we need to be
thinking about that mantra,

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:14.960
which we keep hearing again and again

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:18.600
which is physical distance
does not equal social distance.

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:20.660
And so we need to be giving our children

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:22.550
these nurturing environments

00:11:22.550 --> 00:11:27.510
where they can develop not
as normatively as possible.

00:11:27.510 --> 00:11:30.110
And when we talk about social interaction,

00:11:30.110 --> 00:11:34.050
technology should not be
excluded from that conversation.

00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:37.077
As one of the co-directors of the CIFAR

00:11:37.077 --> 00:11:38.930
and Childhood Brain Development
Program talks about,

00:11:38.930 --> 00:11:42.400
Dr. Candice Odgers, that
technology is not all bad

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:44.370
as the bad rap that it gets.

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:46.440
It can be used for positivity.

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:49.080
And in this case, it is to be used

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:51.610
to maybe a form those social groups

00:11:51.610 --> 00:11:55.450
that children don't have
when they're not in school.

00:11:55.450 --> 00:11:57.099
Finally, I'll just say on this point

00:11:57.099 --> 00:11:59.730
of nurturing environments is that pods

00:11:59.730 --> 00:12:02.220
could serve as a really
nurturing environment.

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:04.280
I know they've gotten a really bad rap

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:06.700
because we talk about being exclusive

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:10.770
and perpetuating the inequities
that we find in society.

00:12:10.770 --> 00:12:13.470
So our goal is to create those pods

00:12:13.470 --> 00:12:15.400
as nurturing environments

00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:18.870
but to challenge ourselves
as families to be inclusive

00:12:18.870 --> 00:12:21.480
and include families who
might not necessarily

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:23.422
be part of those pods.

00:12:23.422 --> 00:12:25.430
So that was nurturing environments

00:12:25.430 --> 00:12:26.780
being able to reverse

00:12:26.780 --> 00:12:29.910
all these generational legacies of trauma.

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:31.100
Then, there's the importance

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:34.040
of parental behavior and caregiving.

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:38.180
And for this are pointed to
the work of Michael Meaney

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:39.900
and Kieran O'Donnell who are, again,

00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:42.650
members of the CIFAR Childhood
Brain Development Program

00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:46.100
where maternal care and
parental care in general

00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:48.720
is a big component by which children

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:51.790
learn safe versus unsafe environments

00:12:51.790 --> 00:12:54.130
and learn how to buffer their emotions.

00:12:54.130 --> 00:12:57.644
And so at a time when
there's a lot of stress

00:12:57.644 --> 00:13:00.568
placed on parents because
of the limited resources

00:13:00.568 --> 00:13:03.950
of their time and having
to balance homeschooling

00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:05.943
versus other responsibilities,

00:13:07.141 --> 00:13:08.740
it's challenging for the parents,

00:13:08.740 --> 00:13:10.660
but the parents need
to be able to reach out

00:13:10.660 --> 00:13:12.220
to support groups as well

00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:14.560
to be able to aid them

00:13:14.560 --> 00:13:18.240
in being the best parents that they can be

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:20.560
at a really challenging time.

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:23.440
And so the last thing I'll say

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:24.640
in answer to that question

00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:26.610
is how do we become better parents

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:29.580
and teach our children to
weather the storm that is COVID

00:13:29.580 --> 00:13:31.930
and really anything
that's thrown out them?

00:13:31.930 --> 00:13:34.730
And for this, I'll take
a page out of things

00:13:34.730 --> 00:13:37.180
that I've learned from
the CIFAR CBD program

00:13:37.180 --> 00:13:40.260
but a lot from the Tibetan
Buddhist monks and nuns

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:42.450
that I've taught over the years

00:13:42.450 --> 00:13:45.180
in that empathy, kindness, and compassion

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:49.190
need to be fostered in our communities,

00:13:49.190 --> 00:13:51.360
and that will allow us to buffer

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:53.620
our generational legacies of trauma

00:13:53.620 --> 00:13:57.130
in the generation that's directly
exposed, but also future.

00:13:57.130 --> 00:13:58.780
Now, you might be saying, "But empathy,

00:13:58.780 --> 00:14:01.840
kindness, and compassionate
ar extremely esoteric terms.

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:04.080
And how do we actually inculcate them?"

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:08.070
And there's a Heldy literature
on social-emotional learning,

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:10.050
a lot of it based in Canada,

00:14:10.050 --> 00:14:13.170
which has been rolled out
into curriculum in schools.

00:14:13.170 --> 00:14:16.270
And so these traits can be taught.

00:14:16.270 --> 00:14:18.100
And in teaching those traits

00:14:18.100 --> 00:14:20.610
both to the children, the teachers,

00:14:20.610 --> 00:14:21.890
as well as the parents,

00:14:21.890 --> 00:14:24.210
we can create cohesive societies

00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:27.000
that will allow us to weather a COVID,

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:31.187
but also future storms
that might come our way.

00:14:31.187 --> 00:14:34.310
- Brian, there's an audience
question that's come in

00:14:34.310 --> 00:14:36.713
that's a perfect follow up to this point.

00:14:37.700 --> 00:14:42.010
They're asking, "Given where in the midst

00:14:42.010 --> 00:14:44.040
of the COVID-19 pandemic

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:47.883
and it doesn't seem to be
going away anytime soon,

00:14:50.330 --> 00:14:52.940
one could that it is causing trauma

00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:54.490
and traumatic experiences.

00:14:54.490 --> 00:14:58.160
So what are some practical
evidence-informed steps

00:14:58.160 --> 00:15:02.840
that we can take to protect
children from lasting damage?"

00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:03.673
- Right.

00:15:03.673 --> 00:15:05.990
So some of the evidence based steps side

00:15:05.990 --> 00:15:08.220
are you need supportive relationships.

00:15:08.220 --> 00:15:10.080
And so what those supportive relationships

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:12.460
for children are are their parents,

00:15:12.460 --> 00:15:15.800
if there are parents to
give to nurture them,

00:15:15.800 --> 00:15:19.330
or an immediate community that allows them

00:15:19.330 --> 00:15:22.210
to not feel socially isolated

00:15:22.210 --> 00:15:23.520
because at the end of the day

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:26.450
we are social beings and
for them to be stripped away

00:15:26.450 --> 00:15:30.050
from that social fabric that
really knits them together

00:15:30.050 --> 00:15:32.880
in their community is
extremely jarring for children.

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:36.160
And so to try and create
that social fabric again

00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:37.530
in whatever shape or form.

00:15:37.530 --> 00:15:40.665
So the pods are one of these possibilities

00:15:40.665 --> 00:15:45.520
that allow for that aspect
to be able to be buffered.

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:50.520
Then there's the physical
benefits of exercise, for example,

00:15:50.930 --> 00:15:54.016
and you have children who are
now cooped up in the house

00:15:54.016 --> 00:15:58.970
with no outlet sometimes
for physical activity

00:15:58.970 --> 00:16:00.830
or we know that physical activity

00:16:00.830 --> 00:16:04.460
releases neurochemical that are important

00:16:04.460 --> 00:16:05.780
for brain development,

00:16:05.780 --> 00:16:09.280
releases neurochemical
that allow for empathy

00:16:09.280 --> 00:16:13.191
and for (indistinct) of
mind-related practices

00:16:13.191 --> 00:16:14.687
to get firmed up,

00:16:14.687 --> 00:16:17.730
that is being able to form
relationships without us.

00:16:17.730 --> 00:16:21.680
And so to get out as best as
possible into wide open areas

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:23.620
like parks that are available,

00:16:23.620 --> 00:16:26.150
to be able to allow the kids to engage

00:16:26.150 --> 00:16:27.507
in that physical activity.

00:16:27.507 --> 00:16:29.800
And one might also

00:16:31.100 --> 00:16:33.440
run into and encounter
other families there

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:35.660
and that would give them
that social interaction

00:16:35.660 --> 00:16:37.180
that they crave as well.

00:16:37.180 --> 00:16:41.270
And we really need to be looking also

00:16:41.270 --> 00:16:45.440
into nutritional aspects
of child development

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:49.330
because we know that there
are these times where schools,

00:16:49.330 --> 00:16:50.780
at least in the United States schools,

00:16:50.780 --> 00:16:53.680
who are a big source of
food for a lot of children,

00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:55.100
and now those have gone away,

00:16:55.100 --> 00:16:59.100
how do we really get appropriate
nutrition to children?

00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:01.660
And so those are some
things which we can do

00:17:01.660 --> 00:17:05.229
to really be able to buffer
the detrimental influences

00:17:05.229 --> 00:17:08.530
of the COVID situation.

00:17:08.530 --> 00:17:11.760
And as I said, let's
not rule out technology.

00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:14.550
Grandparents, parents, uncles, aunts

00:17:14.550 --> 00:17:16.700
are just now Zoom call away.

00:17:16.700 --> 00:17:19.370
And while that might not be the same thing

00:17:19.370 --> 00:17:21.770
as being able to physically
interact with them,

00:17:21.770 --> 00:17:23.790
just seeing those familiar faces,

00:17:23.790 --> 00:17:26.180
just being able to interact with them

00:17:26.180 --> 00:17:30.850
is going to really not only
influence brain development,

00:17:30.850 --> 00:17:33.650
but it's also going to be
able to allow them to titrate

00:17:33.650 --> 00:17:36.120
and collaborate their social relationships

00:17:36.120 --> 00:17:38.170
at a time where they're not getting them.

00:17:39.860 --> 00:17:41.560
- Thanks, Brian.

00:17:41.560 --> 00:17:44.770
So another question that's
come in from the audience

00:17:44.770 --> 00:17:49.630
is maybe taking a turn
back to your own research.

00:17:49.630 --> 00:17:54.630
This person's asking
about norms in the field.

00:17:55.050 --> 00:17:58.930
So, "Research in this
field tends to be done

00:17:58.930 --> 00:18:02.263
in animal models, whether
it mice or primates-wise,

00:18:03.210 --> 00:18:05.383
fruit flies, honeybees, et cetera.

00:18:06.527 --> 00:18:10.251
How do we know that these
are relevant to humans?

00:18:10.251 --> 00:18:12.417
How do you make bridging that gap?"

00:18:12.417 --> 00:18:14.540
- Right, and so thank
you for that question.

00:18:14.540 --> 00:18:17.980
In fact, a lot of my research
has been firmly rooted

00:18:17.980 --> 00:18:19.900
in the basic science of understanding

00:18:19.900 --> 00:18:22.182
how intergenerational legacies of stress

00:18:22.182 --> 00:18:26.010
get inherited or socially
transmitted across generations.

00:18:26.010 --> 00:18:29.840
And so those studies
are extremely important

00:18:29.840 --> 00:18:32.270
because they allow us to control

00:18:32.270 --> 00:18:34.820
for variables that can't be in controlled

00:18:34.820 --> 00:18:36.000
in the human condition.

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:40.970
And so we now know that using
mice, using nonhuman primates,

00:18:40.970 --> 00:18:42.550
using flies, using worms

00:18:42.550 --> 00:18:45.400
that there are general
principles that might suggest

00:18:45.400 --> 00:18:47.710
that what happens in one generation

00:18:47.710 --> 00:18:49.370
influences that generation

00:18:49.370 --> 00:18:52.900
but also can inform

00:18:52.900 --> 00:18:55.210
how the future generations
are going to process

00:18:55.210 --> 00:18:56.310
their own environments.

00:18:56.310 --> 00:18:58.253
And so we know that at this point.

00:19:00.659 --> 00:19:03.050
What we don't know in the human condition

00:19:03.050 --> 00:19:06.820
is how much of that is
inherited by the sperm and egg.

00:19:06.820 --> 00:19:10.350
How much of that is actually
the maternal fetal interface

00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:12.140
when that is actually in play,

00:19:12.140 --> 00:19:14.410
and how much of that
is social transmission?

00:19:14.410 --> 00:19:17.253
Those are incredibly
difficult to rule out.

00:19:19.770 --> 00:19:21.030
Studying all of this,

00:19:21.030 --> 00:19:26.030
I am mechanistically driven
to understand how this occurs,

00:19:26.300 --> 00:19:28.530
but from a phenomenological level

00:19:28.530 --> 00:19:31.280
if we don't understand this
from a human perspective

00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:34.990
the fact that it occurs is unquestioned.

00:19:34.990 --> 00:19:37.800
How it occurs is what
we're thinking about.

00:19:37.800 --> 00:19:40.510
And so if the idea I keep the motivation

00:19:40.510 --> 00:19:43.930
in front of me to beat, to reverse,

00:19:43.930 --> 00:19:46.230
or to halt these legacies of trauma,

00:19:46.230 --> 00:19:47.950
and at this point,

00:19:47.950 --> 00:19:50.620
that is the central question
that we wanna answer.

00:19:50.620 --> 00:19:55.130
And the answer to that, I
think, will be independent

00:19:55.130 --> 00:19:57.400
of whether it was social transmission,

00:19:57.400 --> 00:19:59.700
whether it was inherited
via sperm and egg,

00:19:59.700 --> 00:20:02.710
or whether it was through
the maternal fetal interface.

00:20:02.710 --> 00:20:04.950
And so I keep that as our motivation

00:20:04.950 --> 00:20:05.960
and our ambition

00:20:05.960 --> 00:20:09.450
and I see no conflict in
wanting to understand mechanism

00:20:09.450 --> 00:20:11.450
but also wanting to intervene.

00:20:11.450 --> 00:20:13.570
And that's one of the
reasons why I just moved

00:20:13.570 --> 00:20:15.330
to the USC Keck School of Medicine

00:20:15.330 --> 00:20:17.170
and Children's Hospital, Los Angeles,

00:20:17.170 --> 00:20:20.130
because I would like to bridge my work

00:20:20.130 --> 00:20:22.280
in the animal systems that I study

00:20:22.280 --> 00:20:26.370
with human-relevant experiences

00:20:26.370 --> 00:20:29.290
to be able to not only
understand mechanism

00:20:29.290 --> 00:20:31.000
but also intervention

00:20:31.000 --> 00:20:34.370
because as mechanistic driven as I am,

00:20:34.370 --> 00:20:37.790
I also understand that
intervention is equally important

00:20:37.790 --> 00:20:39.703
and those are things that we're
trying to really understand

00:20:39.703 --> 00:20:41.660
at this point.

00:20:41.660 --> 00:20:45.150
- I love this next
question that's come in.

00:20:45.150 --> 00:20:48.470
Knowing what you do about this
important area of research

00:20:48.470 --> 00:20:51.680
and all that is 2020,

00:20:51.680 --> 00:20:54.150
how has your own approach to parenting

00:20:54.150 --> 00:20:55.500
either changed or been affected?

00:20:55.500 --> 00:20:57.150
Have you noticed any differences?

00:20:59.720 --> 00:21:00.720
- So the first thing,

00:21:00.720 --> 00:21:03.404
I have noticed differences

00:21:03.404 --> 00:21:06.370
but again, I'll go back to my time

00:21:06.370 --> 00:21:08.270
with the Tibetan Buddhist monks and nuns.

00:21:08.270 --> 00:21:11.030
And so I don't identify as religious,

00:21:11.030 --> 00:21:15.220
but I never wanna divorce the
scientist from the science.

00:21:15.220 --> 00:21:18.700
And so this is something
where I've imbibed

00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:20.170
the mindfulness aspect,

00:21:20.170 --> 00:21:24.860
being present in the moment
aspect of my experience

00:21:24.860 --> 00:21:26.640
with the Tibetan Buddhist monks and nuns.

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:28.050
And what I mean by that

00:21:28.050 --> 00:21:31.880
is I try and give myself
grace every single day

00:21:31.880 --> 00:21:33.710
to not have all the answers.

00:21:33.710 --> 00:21:36.060
Some days are gonna be better than others

00:21:36.060 --> 00:21:39.310
but I'm also looking to appreciate

00:21:39.310 --> 00:21:41.430
again, the silver linings

00:21:41.430 --> 00:21:44.150
because it allows me to spend time

00:21:44.150 --> 00:21:47.500
with my son and daughter every single day

00:21:47.500 --> 00:21:49.920
that I normally would not
have when we were just going

00:21:49.920 --> 00:21:51.490
from soccer practice

00:21:51.490 --> 00:21:55.150
to karate, to music, and
going on that roller coaster.

00:21:55.150 --> 00:21:58.380
And so it's afforded me the
opportunity to take a step back

00:21:58.380 --> 00:22:01.870
and really connect with
what I think I have lost,

00:22:01.870 --> 00:22:02.703
which is important,

00:22:02.703 --> 00:22:05.710
which is the familial
ties that we all have.

00:22:05.710 --> 00:22:08.240
And so just giving myself grace

00:22:08.240 --> 00:22:13.240
a lot of being patient with my spouse

00:22:13.610 --> 00:22:16.400
and trying to figure out work schedules

00:22:16.400 --> 00:22:19.420
to allow for both of our
professional careers to flourish

00:22:19.420 --> 00:22:21.050
at this challenging time.

00:22:21.050 --> 00:22:24.080
And also to be able to
talk to our children

00:22:24.080 --> 00:22:26.720
about the fact that these
are challenging times,

00:22:26.720 --> 00:22:28.350
we all have to work together

00:22:28.350 --> 00:22:30.060
and use this as an opportunity,

00:22:30.060 --> 00:22:32.090
even though they don't
have that social fabric

00:22:32.090 --> 00:22:35.470
that I'm talking about to
be able to inculcate in them

00:22:35.470 --> 00:22:37.510
some element of impulse control,

00:22:37.510 --> 00:22:40.390
some element of realizing that
we're all in this together,

00:22:40.390 --> 00:22:41.977
and that the only way we're
going to come out of this

00:22:41.977 --> 00:22:45.950
is if all of us work as a single unit.

00:22:45.950 --> 00:22:48.750
Some days are better than
others, I will admit.

00:22:48.750 --> 00:22:49.660
But at the same time

00:22:49.660 --> 00:22:52.880
it's about giving myself grace
that I've come to appreciate

00:22:52.880 --> 00:22:54.780
that that's really important.

00:22:54.780 --> 00:22:57.653
- So this will probably
be our last question.

00:22:57.653 --> 00:23:00.223
We're just about out of time,

00:23:01.360 --> 00:23:03.743
but going in a slightly
different direction.

00:23:04.860 --> 00:23:06.420
Can you talk a little bit

00:23:06.420 --> 00:23:08.516
about how cross cultural differences

00:23:08.516 --> 00:23:10.393
might factor into,

00:23:10.393 --> 00:23:15.393
and repercussions of
intergenerational trauma?

00:23:15.410 --> 00:23:17.170
This person essentially asking

00:23:18.580 --> 00:23:20.450
they imagined that that different cultures

00:23:20.450 --> 00:23:23.170
either adapt to or experience
or allow the experience

00:23:23.170 --> 00:23:26.020
of trauma to impact an
individual in different ways.

00:23:26.020 --> 00:23:28.220
And so is there a body of work

00:23:28.220 --> 00:23:32.120
that's taking into account
across cultural differences?

00:23:32.120 --> 00:23:33.850
- Thank you for that
really important question.

00:23:33.850 --> 00:23:36.942
And being an immigrant in
the United States myself,

00:23:36.942 --> 00:23:39.160
it's something that I think about a lot

00:23:39.160 --> 00:23:40.910
as I navigate my own space.

00:23:40.910 --> 00:23:42.766
And so I grew up in India,

00:23:42.766 --> 00:23:44.810
which is a culture

00:23:44.810 --> 00:23:47.930
and you can't generalize India
because there's no one India,

00:23:47.930 --> 00:23:52.450
but if I were to even do that

00:23:52.450 --> 00:23:56.430
it would be one that has a deterministic,

00:23:56.430 --> 00:23:58.833
it puts a lot of emphasis on destiny.

00:24:00.127 --> 00:24:02.550
And the flip side of that is having agency

00:24:02.550 --> 00:24:05.100
to be able to change one's fate in life.

00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:07.380
And so I find those two

00:24:07.380 --> 00:24:09.060
to be at two ends of the spectrum,

00:24:09.060 --> 00:24:11.450
to be able to say that
whatever's in my fate

00:24:11.450 --> 00:24:13.850
or whatever is I'm
destined for, will happen.

00:24:13.850 --> 00:24:17.390
And you can think about how a
culture might think about that

00:24:17.390 --> 00:24:20.710
from an intergenerational
legacy of trauma perspective

00:24:20.710 --> 00:24:25.490
versus yes, there was trauma
in one's ancestral environment

00:24:25.490 --> 00:24:27.960
but I have the agency to change that.

00:24:27.960 --> 00:24:29.900
And so that's something which one

00:24:29.900 --> 00:24:32.380
really has to take into account.

00:24:32.380 --> 00:24:34.280
And the only way one
takes that into account

00:24:34.280 --> 00:24:37.840
is by sitting down at tables

00:24:37.840 --> 00:24:40.750
with people who have
those different viewpoints

00:24:40.750 --> 00:24:44.430
and trying to come to some consensus,

00:24:44.430 --> 00:24:46.140
and there might not always be consensus,

00:24:46.140 --> 00:24:49.570
but at least realizing that
these two opposite ends

00:24:49.570 --> 00:24:53.410
of the spectrum allows one
to be able to be empathetic

00:24:53.410 --> 00:24:55.700
to the other side, other point of view.

00:24:55.700 --> 00:24:59.890
But the science is firmly only
on the side of having agency,

00:24:59.890 --> 00:25:03.130
and that agency does not only
have to come from oneself.

00:25:03.130 --> 00:25:04.890
It could come from the community.

00:25:04.890 --> 00:25:06.910
It could come from the social workers

00:25:06.910 --> 00:25:09.293
that are pioneers in the work

00:25:09.293 --> 00:25:11.410
that is done to reverse

00:25:11.410 --> 00:25:13.320
intergenerational legacies of trauma.

00:25:13.320 --> 00:25:16.360
But there is an agency in our communities

00:25:16.360 --> 00:25:19.920
in our individuals to be able
to change and halt legacies

00:25:19.920 --> 00:25:23.350
of stress from perpetuating
across generations.

00:25:23.350 --> 00:25:24.553
- One last question.

00:25:24.553 --> 00:25:25.386
(laughing)

00:25:25.386 --> 00:25:26.593
We do have time.

00:25:27.575 --> 00:25:28.408
This person wants to know,

00:25:28.408 --> 00:25:30.820
and apologies in advance

00:25:30.820 --> 00:25:33.550
if this is maybe getting a
little bit too technical, folks,

00:25:33.550 --> 00:25:35.350
but what is the mechanism

00:25:35.350 --> 00:25:38.099
by which the egg and
sperm may be affected?

00:25:38.099 --> 00:25:40.700
So in other words, how do these
traumas in the environment

00:25:40.700 --> 00:25:42.233
actually affect our genes?

00:25:43.490 --> 00:25:44.950
- So within all of us,

00:25:44.950 --> 00:25:48.020
there are molecules called
readers, writers, and erasers,

00:25:48.020 --> 00:25:51.360
and what these readers,
writers, and erasers are doing

00:25:51.360 --> 00:25:55.210
is they're laying down the
equivalent of punctuations,

00:25:55.210 --> 00:25:56.960
like full stops or comma

00:25:56.960 --> 00:26:00.420
or highlights on our books
of life, which is our DNA.

00:26:00.420 --> 00:26:03.270
And so you can think of
the environmental event

00:26:03.270 --> 00:26:05.270
whatever environmental event that is,

00:26:05.270 --> 00:26:08.420
that environmental event
is then putting down

00:26:08.420 --> 00:26:10.710
the equivalent of punctuations,

00:26:10.710 --> 00:26:12.700
DNA methylation being one of them,

00:26:12.700 --> 00:26:15.790
RNA modifications being another,

00:26:15.790 --> 00:26:19.040
histone modifications being
yet another punctuation.

00:26:19.040 --> 00:26:21.630
And what these punctuations
do is then they allow

00:26:21.630 --> 00:26:25.270
for genes, our DNA to
either be read or not.

00:26:25.270 --> 00:26:27.180
They allow for them at book of life

00:26:27.180 --> 00:26:31.760
either to be read at that space
where there's a punctuation

00:26:31.760 --> 00:26:34.220
or an edit versus not.

00:26:34.220 --> 00:26:35.680
And so what we think is happening

00:26:35.680 --> 00:26:38.350
is that there's a confluence,

00:26:38.350 --> 00:26:41.033
a combination, of how DNA methylation,

00:26:41.033 --> 00:26:43.413
RNA modifications, and histone

00:26:43.413 --> 00:26:45.620
are found in sperm and egg

00:26:45.620 --> 00:26:48.420
that then biases embryonic development

00:26:48.420 --> 00:26:51.630
to roll down one side of a
hill to give, for example,

00:26:51.630 --> 00:26:54.200
a mental health state that is detrimental

00:26:54.200 --> 00:26:56.390
or to roll down another side of the hill

00:26:56.390 --> 00:26:59.210
where that mental state does not manifest.

00:26:59.210 --> 00:27:02.015
And so we want to think
about the DNA methylation,

00:27:02.015 --> 00:27:03.640
the histone modifications,

00:27:03.640 --> 00:27:07.160
and the RNA found in sperm
and egg as punctuations

00:27:07.160 --> 00:27:09.570
that allows the book of
life to be read differently.

00:27:09.570 --> 00:27:12.460
And as orchestrators of gene expression

00:27:12.460 --> 00:27:15.671
that then allow for the
multicellular UNI to form

00:27:15.671 --> 00:27:18.550
in rolling down a hill,

00:27:18.550 --> 00:27:21.310
on one side, mental health
states being prevalent

00:27:21.310 --> 00:27:23.050
on one side, the resilience

00:27:23.050 --> 00:27:25.393
to those mental health
states from developing.

00:27:26.770 --> 00:27:27.890
- Brian, thank you so much

00:27:27.890 --> 00:27:30.530
for taking the time to
answer our questions.

00:27:30.530 --> 00:27:32.440
It's been a pleasure
to hear about your work

00:27:32.440 --> 00:27:34.800
in the context of COVID.

00:27:34.800 --> 00:27:37.070
And I just wanted to again thank you

00:27:37.070 --> 00:27:40.240
and wish you the best of luck at USC

00:27:40.240 --> 00:27:43.190
as you get settled in Los Angeles.

00:27:43.190 --> 00:27:44.023
